steps.body:
A sorted list rotated at some point, like `[40, 50, 60, 10, 20, 30]`, is no longer sorted, so a plain binary search can throw away the half that holds the target. But cut any range in the middle and at least one half is still in order. Find that half by comparing its two ends, check whether the target lies between them, and keep whichever half can hold the target:

```python
def search_rotated(nums, target):
    lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1
    while lo <= hi:
        mid = (lo + hi) // 2
        if nums[mid] == target:
            return mid
        if nums[lo] <= nums[mid]:  # the left half is in order
            if nums[lo] <= target < nums[mid]:
                hi = mid - 1
            else:
                lo = mid + 1
        else:  # the right half is in order
            if nums[mid] < target <= nums[hi]:
                lo = mid + 1
            else:
                hi = mid - 1
    return -1


print(search_rotated([40, 50, 60, 10, 20, 30], 20))  # 4
```

The chained comparison `nums[lo] <= target < nums[mid]` reads like the range it tests. When the target isn't in the half that is in order, it can only be in the other half, break and all. Each pass still drops half the range: O(log n) time and O(1) extra space.

Keep the `<=` in `nums[lo] <= nums[mid]`. When `lo` and `mid` are the same index, that one-item half is in order, and `<` sends the search the wrong way. This also assumes no value repeats. In `[1, 3, 1, 1, 1]` the ends of a half can be equal around a break, and the search misses the 3.

